<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giacomo da Lentini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1230–<a href="/years/1240">1240</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Jacopo da Lentini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giacomo da Lentini was a notary at the court of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> and the leading poet of the Sicilian School, which wrote love lyrics in a refined Sicilian vernacular. He is usually credited with inventing the sonnet. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> named him as the representative of the school, and its poetry shaped later Italian verse, including that of Dante and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giacomo-da-lentini.jpg" alt="Giacomo da Lentini" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonimo monaco fiorentino (Anonymous Florentine monk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacopo_da_Lentini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
